{:edb/db-before-t 1128 :edb/db-after-t 1129 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534441 50 #inst "2026-10-11T20:48:49.072Z" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186177915 1078 #edb/ref 17592186177919 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186179744 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421762 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186179744 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421763 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186179745 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421760 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186179746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421761 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186179746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421764 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186179867 1050 1200.0 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186180041 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186180041 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = y^{2}i + xyj$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4x^{2} + 9y^{2} = 36$." 13194139534441 17592186045417 false] [17592186180041 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i+xy\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186180041 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $2,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =xy=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+18{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }26{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1}26{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186180728 1064 "Let's now derive the following formula for the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that if $C$ is parameterized by the function $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ then \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{y'(t)\\,\\mathbf i - x'(t)\\,\\mathbf j}{||\\mathbf r'(t)||},\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that $C$ is positively-oriented. \n\nSubstituting our expression for $\\mathbf n$ into our line integral definition of flux and using the fact that $\\textrm d s = ||\\mathbf r'(t)|| \\,\\textrm d t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{'}(t)\\,i-{x}^{'}(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{'}(t)||})||{r}^{'}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{'}(t)\\,i-{x}^{'}(t)\\,j}{\\cancel{||{r}^{'}(t)||}})\\cancel{||{r}^{'}(t)||}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot ({y}^{'}(t)\\,i-{x}^{'}(t)\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P(x(t),y(t)){y}^{'}(t)-Q(x(t),y(t)){x}^{'}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534441 17592186045417 false] [17592186180728 1064 "Let's now derive the following formula for the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that if $C$ is parameterized by the function $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ then \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{y'(t)\\,\\mathbf i - x'(t)\\,\\mathbf j}{||\\mathbf r'(t)||},\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that $C$ is positively-oriented. \n\nSubstituting our expression for $\\mathbf n$ into our line integral definition of flux and using the fact that $\\textrm d s = ||\\mathbf r'(t)|| \\,\\textrm d t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||})||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\cancel{||{r}^{′}(t)||}})\\cancel{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot ({y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P(x(t),y(t)){y}^{′}(t)-Q(x(t),y(t)){x}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421730 1080 "0" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421730 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534441 1759