6421760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421760 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=100.$" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421760 1072 #uuid "3e7323f9-19dc-5d6f-a8c7-1664ab5ee0f5" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421760 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $5$ and $2,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={e}^{y}={e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={e}^{x}={e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitutions $u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,du=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt$ and $v=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,dv=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u+{\\int }_{1}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421755 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421761 1069 "q-127256" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421761 1071 "![](images/62/62c8f6d7db345ce76bd164bb52c13e5edef9c04f18f4f07501c8694e7f64b554.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $x={e}^{y}$ from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(e,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421761 1072 #uuid "735394db-bfa6-5cb2-9d7d-19fe3d01e994" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421761 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(e,1).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/ba/ba6285008fd90835fdbf73203b76a0a01d713889d9939979aaec54bebd0a8c49.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}={t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x}={e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2})\\cdot (1)-({e}^{-t})\\cdot ({e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421762 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421756 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421762 1069 "q-127062" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421762 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421762 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421762 1072 #uuid "90df2f56-7b61-5a49-aa6d-4d0c58b35b14" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421762 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={e}^{y}={e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={e}^{x}={e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitutions $u=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,du=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt$ and $v=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,dv=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}{e}^{u}\\,du-{\\int }_{1}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421763 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421758 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421763 1069 "q-127082" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421763 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421763 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+(x+y)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421763 1072 #uuid "b2007897-e589-5e81-a8ff-d3a65c150dee" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421763 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x+y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+15{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =15(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =30\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421757 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421764 1069 "q-127235" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421764 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421764 1071 "![](images/95/95e6a455863f0b4cefe184cca9ad86feaeff9f0702808db2d151f5d6615a04b8.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i-y\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421764 1072 #uuid "ddff35a9-8792-529b-9827-d26481dbe529" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true] [17592186421764 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/cc/cc9d66f800a57f27673577cd3fd179b4cd6ff64585c5dc1a867317d8d592e485.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y=t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-y=-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})-(-\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534441 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-093b5f1d-7084-5cca-af48-56a7c276ea5d" 17592186421730 "answer-0a0fbaf0-fbaf-5ffb-ba78-e300b9c3cb2c" 17592186421731 "answer-1755e313-29bb-5235-930c-5480eea8e580" 17592186421732 "answer-29d3016e-ea1f-57be-ad3f-89bd52e2b595" 17592186421733 "answer-29d80bea-8d5f-5d6e-9d57-a11322c402b1" 17592186421734 "answer-2a42ddb5-8533-50f3-9230-498c83bae1a9" 17592186421735 "answer-2cd76549-ed5c-561a-a07f-a7506fbab6d4" 17592186421736 "answer-37855248-eec6-54d6-abfc-d67f62c8a619" 17592186421737 "answer-3cf1e009-304c-5363-9a50-24170560d53d" 17592186421738 "answer-527da9e8-31b2-5d12-ada1-f6448689e756" 17592186421739 "answer-54352619-e7d8-51ab-bf81-2466d57fb916" 17592186421740 "answer-5f942441-ff7e-546f-8b6c-c0bc3ed8b008" 17592186421741 "answer-6097d82c-a160-580e-ba98-359ee8f02940" 17592186421742 "answer-77c54161-201d-5420-8d49-397874bb229d" 17592186421743 "answer-8ebff72e-dab2-5efc-93c1-8a98f5c588a0" 17592186421744 "answer-93070110-72d1-593c-aaa7-f43291b04560" 17592186421745 "answer-9a40d272-dad6-5203-a06f-4c1b6242a085" 17592186421746 "answer-a5ebd4b0-5c2f-5c66-823d-14cb8a9a334c" 17592186421747 "answer-b79b1436-2e95-5a91-a8c3-0d137af242fb" 17592186421748 "answer-c7b8c70b-0bf4-5466-85d5-56e8bfa6fc37" 17592186421749 "answer-cb802d80-52ae-51ef-9629-c1c85410bffa" 17592186421750 "answer-d6be670d-4038-5dbc-ac98-7208e5a8c53f" 17592186421751 "answer-dfbe47bf-91a3-5026-b0f2-e265e56e1b7f" 17592186421752 "answer-eb22ea92-b3d9-59e8-9fe5-49b3